Certainly! Below is an example of an HTML and JavaScript page that allows you to rearrange the letters within the magic symbol and substitute one letter for another while maintaining its original placement. This example uses the magic symbol image you provided.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Magic Symbol Rearranger</title>
    <style>
        #symbol {
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 300px;
            width: 300px;
            margin: 0 auto;
        }
        #symbol img {
            max-width: 100%;
            max-height: 100%;
        }
        #letters {
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            flex-wrap: wrap;
            margin-top: 20px;
        }
        #letters input {
            margin: 5px;
            padding: 5px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="symbol">
        <img src="magic_symbol.png" alt="Magic Symbol">
    </div>
    <div id="letters">
        <input type="text" id="letter1" value="A">
        <input type="text" id="letter2" value="B">
        <input type="text" id="letter3" value="C">
        <!-- Add more input fields for each letter in the symbol -->
    </div>
    <button onclick="updateSymbol()">Update Symbol</button>

    <script>
        function updateSymbol() {
            const symbol = document.getElementById('symbol');
            const letters = document.querySelectorAll('#letters input');

            // Replace the letters in the symbol with the new values
            letters.forEach((letter, index) => {
                const letterValue = letter.value;
                symbol.innerHTML = symbol.innerHTML.replace(RegExp(letters[index].value, 'g'), letterValue);
            });
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML Structure:**
   - The `#symbol` div contains the image of the magic symbol.
   - The `#letters` div contains input fields for each letter in the symbol. You need to add an input field for each letter in the symbol.
   - A button is provided to trigger the `updateSymbol` function.

2. **CSS Styling:**
   - The CSS styles the layout of the symbol and the input fields to make them visually appealing.

3. **JavaScript:**
   - The `updateSymbol` function is called when the button is clicked.
   - It iterates over each input field, gets the new value, and replaces the corresponding letter in the symbol with the new value.

### Usage:
- Open the HTML file in a web browser.
- Input the new letters you want to substitute in the input fields.
- Click the "Update Symbol" button to see the changes.

Make sure to replace `"magic_symbol.png"` with the actual path to the image file on your local machine or server.

